00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:11.980
Committee Feedback: Authorisation and Assurance
Lord Victor Adebowale

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.559
Non Executive Director
Highlights

00:00:14.559 --> 00:00:24.950
The paper is pretty clear as to what we have
been up to and why.

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:28.490
There are two things I would draw your attention
to.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:36.350
One is a minor amendment to the Committee's
terms of reference, really around increasing

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:44.129
the scope to look at the development of CCGs,
because we are moving into a slightly different

00:00:44.129 --> 00:00:45.129
phase now.

00:00:45.129 --> 00:00:53.629
CCGs are up and running, so it is about improvement
en mass, continuous improvement, kaizen and

00:00:53.629 --> 00:00:54.930
all that.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:01:03.829
The second is the proposed delegate the intervention
where necessary; as CCGs move into that operational

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:09.620
mode, it may well be necessary for the subcommittee
to intervene, particularly where there are

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:13.510
particular areas of immediate concern.

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:20.070
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, I think we received
that report.

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:21.070
Committee Feedback: Directly Commissioned
Services

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:22.070
Professor Sir Malcolm Grant and Dame Barbara
Hakin

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:23.070
Inaugural meeting overview
The second one is from me, which is the report

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:24.870
of the Directly Commissioned Services Committee.

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:30.660
We had our inaugural meeting in August, in
which we set about reviewing and trying to

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:34.960
rationalise the terms of reference for the
Committee and also the relationship between

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:41.140
the Committee and the Clinical Priorities
Advisory Group, which is a group which will

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:45.190
feed its intelligence back up to the main
Committee.

00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:50.810
We identified the principal areas in relation
to the direct commissioning functions, which

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:55.810
are set out in the brief report in front of
you and we have received various updates in

00:01:55.810 --> 00:02:00.280
relation to primary care and specialised commissioning
from the Heads of Commissioning.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:05.530
I am going to ask Barbara to outline briefly
one of the important decisions that we took

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:11.730
and we reflected on quite carefully, which
is around interim proposals for patient cohort

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:12.730
and individual funding requests.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:13.730
Commissioning exceptions
Individual funding requests

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:17.210
Barbara Hakin: Thank you, Chairman.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:23.040
As with all commissioned services, NHS England
sets out those services which it routinely

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.420
commissions and that we offer for patients
and put contracts in place for.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:35.760
There are, however, times when, for reasons,
we need to make an exception to some of the

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.640
things that we perhaps would not routinely
commission and we have to have a system in

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:44.470
place for doing what are called, 'Individual
funding requests'.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:50.740
To give a couple of examples, that might be
an instance where we may not routinely commission

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:59.930
some sort of surgery which, for the most part,
is cosmetic, but the patient was having severe

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:05.061
health problems because of that, then that
would be a time for an exception; or there

00:03:05.061 --> 00:03:12.460
may be certain treatments or drugs which are
recommended for certain illnesses and not

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:18.600
others, but there may be an exceptional occasion,
particularly at the end of life for patients,

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:22.959
when it would be reasonable to use that drug,
even though it is not routinely used for that

00:03:22.959 --> 00:03:24.330
particular type of illness.

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:27.080
That is very often common with the cancer
drugs.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:29.840
Group exceptions
As such, we have to have in place, across

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:35.930
NHS England, a mechanism for identifying how
we deal with those exceptions.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:43.030
We have a particular difficulty in that when
you are likely to have a group of exceptions

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:47.569
-- when it goes beyond a certain number -- you
need to have a policy in place for how you

00:03:47.569 --> 00:03:49.569
will deal with those patients.

00:03:49.569 --> 00:03:55.080
Of course, we are now one commissioning body,
when there were once 152.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:04:01.440
Consequently, we are in a position where although
we have a comprehensive system of exception

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:06.371
reporting of individual funding requests and
the way we deal with those, there are now

00:04:06.371 --> 00:04:13.090
quite a lot of instances where we need very
specific policies for cohorts of patients

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:18.329
for whom the issue would be the same and who
therefore do not meet the 'exceptional' category.

00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:22.970
Committee decision
In essence, what the Committee decided was

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:27.800
that it was absolutely essential that in the
interim, patients were not denied treatment

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:32.550
if it was clinical necessary and urgent, whilst
we put those policies in place.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.070
It is a big task.

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:37.450
The Committee therefore approved an interim
policy whereby the Area Team Directors would

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:41.610
have the authority to make decisions on that
basis, until we could get to a place where

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:45.889
we were confident that we had all the cohort
policies in place.

00:04:45.889 --> 00:04:49.490
Notwithstanding that, we would still need
to go on with the individual funding requests.

00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:53.611
Supplementary remarks
Malcolm Grant: I hope Members of the Board

00:04:53.611 --> 00:04:54.611
are happy to approve that.

00:04:54.611 --> 00:04:56.880
Our view was that we should simply get on
and authorise it and argue about the money

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:57.880
afterwards.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:01.370
If you are content with that, we will take
that as approval.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:02.370
Thank you.

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:04.910
That is all I have on directly commissioned
services.

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:07.881
Let me, then, move on to Finance and Investment.

00:05:07.881 --> 00:05:09.430
Committee Feedback: Finance and Investment
Dame Moira Gibb

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:10.960
Non Executive Director
Remarks and commendation

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:17.729
Paul may have something to add, but I do not
have anything to add to the paper, which is

00:05:17.729 --> 00:05:18.729
