00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:34.910
That is the 'new'.

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:38.770
The 'old' is that we are picking up, as a
Committee, lots of things that have been in

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:46.620
the system for a very long time and in a sense,
there are sometimes very limited choices for

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:47.889
the Committee to resolve, when it has gone
so far.

00:05:47.889 --> 00:05:48.889
Otherwise, I commend the paper to the Board.

00:05:48.889 --> 00:05:49.889
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Moira.

00:05:49.889 --> 00:05:52.050
Paul, is there anything you would like to
add to that?

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:55.160
Paul Baumann: No, I think it is all in the
paper.

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:57.610
Malcolm Grant: Okay.

00:05:57.610 --> 00:06:03.090
May we, then, move on to the final report,
which is Procurement Controls.

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:04.310
Committee Feedback: Procurement Controls
Paul Baumann

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:05.440
Chief Financial Officer
Summary

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:09.870
This is the new, improved version of the Procurement
Controls Committee report, which has values

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:13.375
attached to it, following the request of the
last meeting.

00:06:13.375 --> 00:06:15.330
You can see the numbers on the following page.

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:20.010
I have to say, this is a Committee which has
an enormous workload simply to keep the business

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:24.060
flowing, given the nature of the procurement
controls which we work under; and I think

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:28.840
the statistics on the page behind, the 79
that we have done in the period since the

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:34.200
last report, gives some measure of the challenge
that we face in that.

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:38.630
We continue to work with the Department of
Health on the right level of procurement controls

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:43.360
and in particular, in pursuit of the earned
autonomy which comes from having the sort

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:47.960
of system which we have put in place to manage
procurement, which will prevent the risk,

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:52.400
if you like, of double controls and therefore
delays and bureaucracy in the system.

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:56.900
We are not quite there yet, but we continue
to work on that and I anticipate that progress

00:06:56.900 --> 00:06:59.400
will be made on that in the next few weeks.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:04.670
Beyond that, it is just for your information
as to what we have been up to.

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:06.440
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much.

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:08.940
May we take that report?

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:09.940
Okay.

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:10.940
Thank you.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:13.560
May we please now move on to item nine, which
is the performance report?

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:19.169
It is a very comprehensive document and I
am assuming that it has been widely read by

00:07:19.169 --> 00:07:20.169
Board members.

00:07:20.169 --> 00:07:26.320
I am going to ask Tim to start off, but other
Members of the Executive team have also had

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:29.680
responsibility for different parts, including
Bill, Barbara and Paul.

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:32.850
Tim, would you please kick off?

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:35.060
Performance Report
Tim Kelsey

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:36.060
National Director for Patients and Information
Data challenges

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:37.060
Yes.

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:38.060
This is the second iteration of the Board
report.

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:39.710
We have tried to take account of comments
made in the previous meeting, to go back to

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:45.509
the actions that I did not talk to when we
covered the minutes; for example, giving greater

00:07:45.509 --> 00:07:52.850
ability to look at regional variation and
institutional variation at local organisational

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:56.419
level in the data, where it is possible.

00:07:56.419 --> 00:08:01.780
A number of things still remain beyond our
grasp, however, I am afraid and that is a

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:06.720
system wide issue to do with data and its
frequency, which we are working on.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:13.860
In broad terms, we are still unable, on a
month by month basis, in any meaningful way

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:19.290
to, for example, track clinical outcomes at
a level we would want to, except in very rare

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:20.290
instances.

00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:24.680
You will still see that the section of, as
it were, historic data is large, larger than

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:31.009
the data we are able to manage in relation
to monitoring the scorecard we have set ourselves

00:08:31.009 --> 00:08:33.019
in the business plan.

00:08:33.019 --> 00:08:37.450
That is the bad news, although it has been
and incredibly helpful stimulus really to

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:40.510
putting pressure on getting better data out.

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:43.950
Data progress
The good news is that, having said all that,

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:48.690
we have actually produced a whole bunch of
new data which, I think, does really help

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:52.920
us start to frame the way in which we are
considering our performance.

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:56.050
The first of those is, obviously, the friends
and family test.

00:08:56.050 --> 00:09:03.270
As I mentioned before, response rates across
the service by June were up to over 15% which

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:08.350
is, I think, an exceptional achievement; although,
of course, as I have also said, that is variable

00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:11.360
by provider institution.

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:19.800
Broadly put, in in patient care, the friends
and family test was scoring the NHS at around

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:24.459
70 across England; and on A&amp;E performance,
that was lower, at around 50.

00:09:24.459 --> 00:09:26.320
These are useful baselines.

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:32.420
I think we should not worry too much about
month on month, but I think as we start to

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:37.080
accumulate more volume and response rates
go up, this will be a very important, useful

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:42.970
measure for us, longitudinally as well as
to give us comparison with other industries

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:47.130
and other healthcare systems that use this
as a measure, of which there are a lot.

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:49.680
We are also able to report on cancer waiting
times.

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:54.820
We have included the results of the NHS England
Staff Barometer for the first time and we

00:09:54.820 --> 00:10:00.560
now have a full suite of financial data.

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:03.920
I can take any further questions we have on
the friends and family test, but I feel we

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:06.140
have probably covered that quite fully in
previous comments.

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:10.830
Perhaps I could hand over to Barbara, then,
to cover urgent care; then, after Barbara,

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:18.310
to Bill to speak to some of the business plan
highlights which are in the summary report;

00:10:18.310 --> 00:10:23.360
then, perhaps, on to Jo, to cover off on organisational
health; and finally, to Paul, to cover off

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:26.580
the financial data which is now in the report.

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:30.410
Urgent care
Section C

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:31.410
Barbara Hakin: Thank you, Tim.

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:33.580
May I take everyone to section C, because
we want to look at some of the key areas of

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:36.970
NHS performance that we have included?

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:42.420
As well as the list on here, I would like
to talk for a few minutes about improving

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:48.910
the access to psychological therapies, which
is another major initiative in NHS England

00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:50.500
to which we are absolutely committed.

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:56.130
I would welcome any questions on the slides,
which cover what we have tried to do here

00:10:56.130 --> 00:10:58.170
following the last Board meeting.

00:10:58.170 --> 00:11:01.180
Target delivery
Firstly, you will see that we have put more

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:06.600
information in about the numbers of patients
who are routinely seen in A&amp;E departments

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:15.180
for elective care, because we felt that was
important for understanding the magnitude

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:18.089
of the task which the NHS has.

00:11:18.089 --> 00:11:23.339
We have tried, in a number of the areas, to
give more indication of the spread, rather

00:11:23.339 --> 00:11:24.950
than just one national figure.

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:30.520
For example, on A&amp;E performance, you can see
that we have shown that the vast majority

00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:40.120
of organisations do meet the 95% standard
and a very, very small number are a long way

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:46.040
adrift from it, with a middle sized number
coming quite close.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:53.610
Again, on the 18 week activity and the Ambulance
Trust activity, we have tried to break this

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:57.350
down to show the range that we get.

00:11:57.350 --> 00:12:04.890
Again, if I can move you on to the 18 week
-- which is page seven -- you will see that

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:12.120
there is quite a significant variation by
Trust as to how well they meet the standards

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:14.260
of seeing patients.

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:18.290
If you remember, the standard is about making
sure the patient receives treatment within

00:12:18.290 --> 00:12:24.980
18 weeks of being referred by their GP; and
you can see that many Trusts do this very

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:32.399
well, but we have a tail of those that really
are not delivering that standard of care for

00:12:32.399 --> 00:12:33.399
patients.

00:12:33.399 --> 00:12:38.980
111
One other thing I would highlight is the 111,

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:41.430
which is on page five.

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:49.180
I think you will see just what a dramatic
improvement there has been in 111 performance,

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:53.990
particularly if you recognise that we have
moved from 20% coverage of the country to

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:56.120
90% coverage of the country.

00:12:56.120 --> 00:13:05.649
The graph on the left shows that actually,
people are really meeting the quality indicators

00:13:05.649 --> 00:13:09.920
for this service absolutely consistently and
calls are being answered very quickly.

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:15.579
We have a really great satisfaction rate:
92% of patients satisfied with the service

00:13:15.579 --> 00:13:16.839
they are getting from 111.

00:13:16.839 --> 00:13:24.800
One of the most important ones is that apart
from a small number of calls, even if they

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:29.980
have to be passed on to a clinician, the vast
majority are being passed on to the clinician

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:33.800
immediately; patients are not waiting to be
rung back, except in a small number of cases.

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:39.130
It is still not good and we will continue
to try to improve this so that all patients

00:13:39.130 --> 00:13:40.420
are dealt with immediately.

00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:43.360
NHS Direct transitioning
I would like to reassure the Board that -- as

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:48.670
everyone is aware -- NHS Direct indicated
that it felt it could no longer provide this

00:13:48.670 --> 00:13:49.670
service.

00:13:49.670 --> 00:13:54.200
We are having very constructive discussions
with alternative providers; dominantly our

00:13:54.200 --> 00:14:01.070
Ambulance Trusts, but often working in partnership
with out of hours providers.

00:14:01.070 --> 00:14:06.019
The CCGs who commission this service I know
are close to being in a position to sign new

00:14:06.019 --> 00:14:11.730
contracts with the Ambulance Trusts and the
other cohort of providers for them to be able

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:13.420
to take over the service.

00:14:13.420 --> 00:14:20.100
Again, we are managing that very carefully
and absolutely in line with the Garland Report,

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:25.700
so in every aspect of this transfer and the
way it is dealt with, we will take the lessons

00:14:25.700 --> 00:14:30.320
from the Garland Report and make sure that
we have full checkpoints for any transfer

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:35.930
to a new service and contingency from other
places to cover the transfer and we will put

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:39.190
phasing in place to make sure that that service
stays stable.

00:14:39.190 --> 00:14:43.680
IAPT
May I just finish with IAPT, then see if anyone

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:45.840
has any questions?

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:52.060
This is a really important initiative and
very important to us.

00:14:52.060 --> 00:14:54.700
Question: [Inaudible]
Barbara Hakin: Sorry, it is not in the papers,

00:14:54.700 --> 00:14:55.700
I am afraid.

00:14:55.700 --> 00:15:00.529
I just wanted to give a verbal update on where
we were with improving these therapies.

00:15:00.529 --> 00:15:07.450
It is really important for us in terms of
ensuring that we have parity of esteem between

00:15:07.450 --> 00:15:08.730
physical and mental health.

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:11.840
It is a service which CCGs commission.

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:18.170
There has recently been a change in the way
we collect and report on the data, which does

00:15:18.170 --> 00:15:25.010
look as if we have had a worsening of our
performance or our trajectory to ensure that

00:15:25.010 --> 00:15:30.959
the right number of patients are seen and
treated over the next 18 months.

00:15:30.959 --> 00:15:36.400
We are hoping that the next letter of information,
which comes out in October, will show that

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:40.150
we are back on track to deliver the Government's
targets on this.

00:15:40.150 --> 00:15:47.700
We certainly have a huge range if initiatives
in place in order to support CCGs to deliver

00:15:47.700 --> 00:15:53.860
this service, including strong clinical leadership,
champions at CCG level and a lot of central

00:15:53.860 --> 00:15:59.770
initiatives from Martin McShane and his team
in Bruce's directorate, to ensure that we

00:15:59.770 --> 00:16:03.170
get back on track and we make sure that patients
do have good access to these services.

00:16:03.170 --> 00:16:04.810
Bruce, I do not know whether you want to add
anything on IAPT?

00:16:04.810 --> 00:16:08.360
Supplementary remarks on IAPT
Bruce Keogh: I might just say a few words,

00:16:08.360 --> 00:16:09.959
if you do not mind.

00:16:09.959 --> 00:16:17.660
The case for improving access to psychological
therapies is based on really good economic

00:16:17.660 --> 00:16:24.670
and clinical grounds and we have, I think,
some quite tough implementation targets.

00:16:24.670 --> 00:16:31.199
By the end of 2015, we are meant to have access
of 15% and a recovery rate for people who

00:16:31.199 --> 00:16:34.110
receive the services of 50%.

00:16:34.110 --> 00:16:42.010
We are on schedule for 12.4% access by the
end of 2014 and we have quite a comprehensive

00:16:42.010 --> 00:16:45.380
place to accelerate progress.

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:50.300
The point I think I would like to make is
that with endeavours of this nature, the progress

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:53.170
is seldom linear.

00:16:53.170 --> 00:16:59.839
It tends to accelerate as momentum is gained
and that is not least of all because training

00:16:59.839 --> 00:17:11.350
with therapists is taking time; and secondly,
because one has to roll out, if you like,

00:17:11.350 --> 00:17:18.379
the acknowledgement of the economic and clinical
case at a time when many CCGs and others are

00:17:18.379 --> 00:17:23.489
having to deal with some very difficult and
conflicting clinical priorities.

00:17:23.489 --> 00:17:31.960
Our anticipation is one of optimism in terms
of meeting the targets and we have a comprehensive

00:17:31.960 --> 00:17:35.080
plan for engaging with CCGs.

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:39.090
We are using the strategic clinical networks;
we have a National Clinical Director, who

00:17:39.090 --> 00:17:45.879
is focusing very heavily on this; we have
engaged NHS Improving Quality; and the Department

00:17:45.879 --> 00:17:50.669
of Health, who I have spoken to and they are
engaging with Health Education England, to

00:17:50.669 --> 00:17:55.539
help us on the difficult issue of recruiting
people to deliver the services and training.

00:17:55.539 --> 00:18:00.730
IAPT comment
David Nicholson: I am sure you did not mean

00:18:00.730 --> 00:18:04.410
to do it in that way, Barbara, but when you
started this you said it was a Government

00:18:04.410 --> 00:18:05.410
target.

00:18:05.410 --> 00:18:08.940
I think that is a very interesting way of
putting because in a sense, this is the issue

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:13.259
we have been dealing with over the last period,
where quite a lot of people have seen this

00:18:13.259 --> 00:18:18.359
as a 'Government target' in the sense of a
politically driven target.

00:18:18.359 --> 00:18:22.830
One of the big differences, I think, that
we can make -- and I think Bruce pointed that

00:18:22.830 --> 00:18:27.309
out really well -- relates to the fact that
the way to get this change is to get the engagement

00:18:27.309 --> 00:18:31.320
and clinical leadership and to get people
locally to understand the nature and scale

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:35.919
of the challenge, but also the evidence behind
it to make it happen.

00:18:35.919 --> 00:18:41.999
That is what we have been focusing our attention
on over the last few months: how can we create

00:18:41.999 --> 00:18:47.059
that understanding in clinical/patient leadership
at a local level to make it happen?

00:18:47.059 --> 00:18:48.299
That takes time and effort.

00:18:48.299 --> 00:18:53.080
It is not a question of setting out a trajectory
and issuing instructions; that is not the

00:18:53.080 --> 00:18:57.639
way you get the kind of clinical change that
you need here to make it happen.

00:18:57.639 --> 00:19:01.840
All credit to Bruce and to Martin and his
team that they have tried to take, I think,

00:19:01.840 --> 00:19:06.970
a very different approach, much more consistent
with the way in which we operate as an organisation,

00:19:06.970 --> 00:19:09.259
to make this really important thing go forward.

00:19:09.259 --> 00:19:14.570
I share with Bruce: I think we should be confident
that we can make this happen.

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:15.970
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:19:15.970 --> 00:19:16.970
Let us move of.

00:19:16.970 --> 00:19:16.976
Barbara, is that all from you?

